<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptahhotep</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 2400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ptahhotep I, Ptahhotpe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptahhotep was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> during the reign of Djedkare Isesi in the Fifth Dynasty, holding the highest administrative office under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and posts such as overseer of the treasury and overseer of all royal works. He is best known as the named author of The Maxims of Ptahhotep, an early work of Egyptian wisdom literature in which a father advises his son on conduct, humility, restraint in speech and respect for Maat. Its oldest surviving copies are in Middle Egyptian, and some scholars argue that it was composed in the Middle Kingdom and that its authorship is fictional. He was buried in Mastaba D62 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptahhotep.jpg" alt="Ptahhotep" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kurohito · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statuevizir095.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…regarded as its masterpiece; the Tale of the Shipwrecked Sailor; the Eloquent Peasant; the dialogue of a man weary of life with his soul; and instructions in wisdom, such as the Teaching of <b>Ptahhotep</b> and the Satire of the Trades, which praises the scribe’s profession above all others.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
